=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0, router1, router2, router3, router4, router5, router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The OSPF routing daemon (`ospfd`) within the FRR (Free Range Routing) stack on every routed device in the topology.

**Mechanism:** Each router's `/etc/frr/daemons` configuration file has `ospfd=no`, which instructs the FRR init system never to start the OSPF daemon. Without `ospfd` running, no OSPF Hello packets are sent, no adjacencies form, and no Link State Advertisements (LSAs) are exchanged. As a result, each router's RIB contains only directly-connected routes. When a host such as pc4 (172.16.10.x) sends traffic toward a remote subnet (e.g., 192.168.200.0/x), its gateway router0 has no matching route and returns an **ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable"** — confirmed by live probe. All other cross-subnet paths time out silently for the same reason.

**Localization:** All seven routers (router0 through router6) share this fault, confirmed by the `ospf_snapshot` helper (zero neighbors, no control socket `ospfd_vty=no` on each) and verified by direct inspection of `/etc/frr/daemons` on router0 showing `ospfd=no`.

**Root cause:** The OSPF daemon is explicitly disabled (`ospfd=no`) in the FRR daemons file on all routers, preventing the control plane from establishing routing adjacencies and distributing inter-subnet routes.

**Fix:** On each router (router0–router6), edit `/etc/frr/daemons` and change `ospfd=no` to `ospfd=yes`, then restart FRR (`service frr restart`). This will bring up ospfd, allow OSPF adjacencies to re-form, and restore full inter-subnet reachability.
=== END ===